# stacked-grids.rq — a stack of tables on one page, named (R74; spec
# 2026-10-28-r74-stacked-grids-design.md § 4.2 as corrected by § 11.2 F1 and F4).
#
# GATE (CLAUDE.md §8): AXIOM, derivation form. Which tables form a stack is declarative over
# facts already in the merged document graph — tab:continuesTable, tab:hasCell, the explicit
# rdf:type tab:EntryCell, and tab:onPage. No geometry, no tuned constant, no numeric literal;
# the only comparison is page EQUALITY, made as a shared-variable join on ?page.
#
# OPEN WORLD, evidence-positive. A stack is grown only from a tab:continuesTable link that is
# PRESENT between two tables whose entry cells share one tab:onPage value. Absence of a link
# never mints, never removes, and never closes anything. The one closed-world guard — the
# head test, "no same-page predecessor" — is query-local and closes WITHIN THE PAGE: the head
# is the table with a same-page successor and no same-page predecessor. The graph stays open.
# The guard is written as MINUS over the (head, page) rows rather than FILTER NOT EXISTS. With
# ?head and ?page always bound on both sides the two are equivalent here; the difference is
# cost. rdflib 7.6 evaluates a MINUS's right side ONCE (evaluate.py evalMinus), where a FILTER
# NOT EXISTS re-runs its pattern for every left row.
#
# A TABLE'S PAGE IS THE PAGE OF ITS tab:EntryCells, never of any cell (spec § 11.2 F1; ruled
# 2026-10-29, Q1 arm (a)). A continuation table carries the head page's tab:LabelCells:
# graincorp-stem's p1#htable1 holds 17 LabelCells on page 0 and 825 EntryCells on page 1, so
# reading "any cell" would put a cross-page link on page 0 and mint a stack that does not
# exist. The type test is an EXPLICIT `a tab:EntryCell`: this runs on the merged graph with no
# subclass closure, and the producers type entry cells explicitly. The membrane
# (tab:StackedGridsShape, K3) closes over the same notion of page.
#
# MEMBERS. The head, plus every table reaching it by tab:continuesTable+ whose entry cells lie
# on the head's page. A chain that crosses pages contributes only its same-page runs, each with
# its own head.
#
# F4 IS RELIED ON, NOT RE-PROVEN. Chains never fork and pages never decrease along a chain
# (F4: document.py's stitching loop refuses to fork the successor map; loop M pairs only page
# N-1 with page N), so the member set is one CONTIGUOUS same-page run. This query does not
# check that. A hypothetical chain H(p1) <- X(p2) <- Y(p1) would make Y a member of H's stack
# across a gap, and K3 would not see it, because every entry cell is on p1. F4 is what rules
# that out, not this query.
#
# A TABLE ON TWO PAGES belongs to K3, not to this query. If a table's entry cells span two
# pages, it can be a member of one page's stack and the head of another's. If it heads runs on
# two pages, both runs mint the same "?head-stack" IRI and merge into one stack across pages.
# tab:StackedGridsShape K3 refuses such a stack (its members' entry cells are not on one page);
# the query does not try to split it. Pinned by tests (h) and (i).
#
# COST: NEAR-LINEAR IN ENTRY CELLS. A table holds hundreds of entry cells (cbh: 170/268/228/84),
# so a join made at cell level multiplies rows per page-equality join. CONSTRUCT dedupes the
# OUTPUT, not the cost, and an earlier draft measured 131.8 s at 20 cells per table. So every
# table is reduced ONCE to its DISTINCT (table, page) rows, in a sub-SELECT, before any
# table-to-table join. Each sub-SELECT scans the entry cells once. Every later join runs over
# (table, page) rows, which number tables x pages, not cells.
#
# The rdflib evaluation is measured, not assumed. A Join with a DISTINCT sub-SELECT child is
# non-lazy (algebra.py analyse): each side is evaluated once and then joined, so no sub-SELECT
# is re-run per left binding. tab:continuesTable* is NOT used for the members: with both ends
# unbound it yields one (n, n) row for every node in the graph, and the non-lazy join's set()
# over those rows was the remaining quadratic term (1.6M FrozenBindings __eq__ calls at 400
# cells per table). The head is emitted from its own row instead, and the members are reached
# with "+", which yields chain pairs only.
#
# OUTPUT, exactly and nothing else: per stack S, (S rdf:type tab:StackedGrids) and one
# (m tab:inStack S) per member, the head included. No label, no provenance triple.
#
# STACK IRI. Minted from the head's IRI with the fixed suffix "-stack":
#     S = IRI(CONCAT(STR(?head), "-stack"))
# so the same input yields the same IRI. The IRI string is only WRITTEN, never read to decide
# anything (spec M7: "same page" is a graph fact, not a name).
#
# Pinned by tests/etkl/test_stacked_grids_query.py.
#
# © 2026 François Rosselet · CC-BY-4.0

PREFIX tab: <https://w3id.org/iladub/tab#>
PREFIX rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>

CONSTRUCT {
  ?stack rdf:type tab:StackedGrids .
  ?head tab:inStack ?stack .
  ?member tab:inStack ?stack .
}
WHERE {
  # 1. HEADS, one row per (head, page). The evidence is a present link whose two tables have
  #    entry cells on one page; each table's pages are reduced to DISTINCT (table, page) rows
  #    before any table-to-table join.
  {
    SELECT DISTINCT ?head ?page WHERE {
      ?succ tab:continuesTable ?head .
      { SELECT DISTINCT ?succ ?page WHERE {
          ?succ tab:hasCell ?succCell .
          ?succCell rdf:type tab:EntryCell ;
                    tab:onPage ?page . } }
      { SELECT DISTINCT ?head ?page WHERE {
          ?head tab:hasCell ?headCell .
          ?headCell rdf:type tab:EntryCell ;
                    tab:onPage ?page . } }
      # The head test: no same-page predecessor. Closed within the page, query-local.
      MINUS {
        ?head tab:continuesTable ?pred .
        { SELECT DISTINCT ?head ?page WHERE {
            ?head tab:hasCell ?hc .
            ?hc rdf:type tab:EntryCell ;
                tab:onPage ?page . } }
        { SELECT DISTINCT ?pred ?page WHERE {
            ?pred tab:hasCell ?predCell .
            ?predCell rdf:type tab:EntryCell ;
                      tab:onPage ?page . } }
      }
    }
  }

  # 2. MEMBERS: the head (emitted from its own row, above) and every table reaching it by
  #    tab:continuesTable+ whose entry cells are on its page. Every head has at least one such
  #    member -- its same-page successor -- so this join drops no head.
  ?member tab:continuesTable+ ?head .
  { SELECT DISTINCT ?member ?page WHERE {
      ?member tab:hasCell ?memberCell .
      ?memberCell rdf:type tab:EntryCell ;
                  tab:onPage ?page . } }

  BIND(IRI(CONCAT(STR(?head), "-stack")) AS ?stack)
}
